<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.BlazorWebAssembly">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <WasmSdkPackageVersion>10.0.7</WasmSdkPackageVersion>
    <RunAOTCompilation>false</RunAOTCompilation>
    <!-- Standards §6.6: the assembly is trim-annotated (IsTrimmable + analyzer), but the app
         publishes untrimmed: the Router's NotFoundPage instantiates components via reflection,
         which the IL trimmer strips in Release publish (CtorNotLocated at runtime → the Blazor
         view tree fails to mount).

         MEASURED 2026-08-23 (Release publish, gzipped — what a browser actually downloads):
           untrimmed  11.99 MB across 219 files
           trimmed     6.18 MB across  96 files   -> 48% smaller
         A runtime audit measured 3.9-11.7 s to first heading across the app's routes, and that
         payload is the reason.

         Radzen.Blazor was added on 2026-08-22 for two components and removed again on
         2026-08-23: it was 1.43 MB gzipped, 11.9% of the entire download, and the second thing
         blocking trimming. The Archive is a semantic .po-table again and the model profile's
         rating curve is inline SVG.

         So the Router's NotFoundPage is now the ONLY blocker. A trimmed publish builds and
         boots — the shell mounts, routing works, the nav renders live API data — but the
         authenticated surface has not been exercised under trimming, and NotFoundPage is
         exactly the kind of thing that fails there. Resolve it (a DynamicDependency attribute
         or an explicit TrimmerRootDescriptor), verify a signed-in session against a trimmed
         build, then flip this on. Do not flip it on the strength of the publish succeeding —
         it succeeds either way. -->
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- Surface trim diagnostics as warnings without failing the build: Blazor-generated Razor code
         and System.Net.Http.Json emit IL2xxx/IL3xxx trim-analyzer warnings that cannot be fixed in
         this app (it ships untrimmed-by-default anyway). Scoped to trim codes only —
         every other warning is still an error per TreatWarningsAsErrors. -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);IL2026;IL2046;IL2057;IL2072;IL2075;IL2087;IL2091;IL2104;IL2110;IL2111;IL3050;IL3051;IL3052;IL3053</WarningsNotAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client" />
    <PackageReference Include="Radzen.Blazor" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PoLocalCompare.Shared\PoLocalCompare.Shared.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Update="wwwroot\models\**\*" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" />
    <!-- Development-only config: enables guest auth in non-Production; never deployed -->
    <Content Update="wwwroot\appsettings.Development.json" CopyToPublishDirectory="Never" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- MEASURED 2026-09-02 (Release publish, gzipped — what a browser actually downloads):
         without Radzen   12.71 MB across 233 files
         with Radzen      15.97 MB across 271 files   -> +3.26 MB, +25.7%
       That is more than double the 1.43 MB the 2026-08-23 removal recorded, because
       Radzen 11.3.0 is larger than the version measured then.

       Of the +3.26 MB, 1.48 MB is Radzen.Blazor.wasm and 1.58 MB is theme CSS this app
       NEVER LOADS — the package publishes all 24 prebuilt themes as static web assets
       regardless of which one is referenced, and index.html references only the structural
       `default-base.css` because the components are painted from our own design tokens.

       An MSBuild target to drop the unreferenced themes was attempted and removed: filtering
       @(StaticWebAsset) after ResolveStaticWebAssetsInputs on PackageId had no effect (the
       publish output was byte-identical), and an inert build target is worse than none. The
       1.58 MB is still recoverable by someone who works out the right item/hook — it would
       take the real cost to ~1.68 MB, close to the historical figure. -->

</Project>
